%S A401067 117,92781,796797,3341637,6725201,12326221,25774633,296765173,
%T A401067 548985177,782481673,883705901,7411737061,7953353109,21573991297,
%U A401067 81870562801,95440494357,305168822857,423644039001,468325170253,496096067881,518112331021,689525754193,1284630973821,1418068645813
%N A401067 Odd numbers m of the form p^(1+4k) * r^2 such that sigma(r^2) divides m, where p is a prime of the form 1+4u, r > 1, and gcd(p,r) = 1.
%C A401067 Numbers that are divisible by the sum of the divisors of their largest unitary divisor that is a square (A401070) and also satisfy Euler's condition for odd perfect numbers (A228058).
%C A401067 The first 270 terms are all deficient (A005100).
%C A401067 The r^2 -portions of these terms all occur in A401387.
%C A401067 Only seven terms out of the initial 270 terms have more than two distinct prime factors, namely: 92781, 548985177, 7953353109, 1284630973821, 347123842547301, 48601262999467737, 17786796207200728461. Applying A350388 to such terms gives the square parts that all must occur in A401388. See also comments in A401386.
%C A401067 Of the initial 270 terms, 267 are in A336547, and only three (548985177, 1284630973821, 48601262999467737) are in A336548.
%H A401067 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A401067/b401067.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..270; terms less than 2^64</a>
%H A401067 <a href="/index/O#opnseqs">Index entries for sequences where odd perfect numbers must occur, if they exist at all</a>.
%e A401067 117 = 3^2 * 13, and sigma(3^2) = 13, which thus divides 117, and therefore 117 is included in this sequence. Note that sigma(117) = 182 = 2*7*13, thus 117 is also a term of A388276.
%e A401067 92781 = 3^2 * 13^2 * 61, sigma(3^2 * 13^2) = 2379 = 3*13*61, which thus divides 92781, and therefore 91781 is a term.
%e A401067 3341637 = 3^2 * 13^5, sigma(3^2) = 13, which thus divides 3341637, and therefore it is included in this sequence. Note that sigma(3341637) = 5229042 = 2*3*7*13*61*157.
%e A401067 548985177 = 3^2 * 13^2 * 61^2 * 97, sigma(3^2 * 13^2 * 61^2) = 8999757 = 3^2 * 13^2 * 61 * 97, which thus divides 548985177, and therefore it is included as a term. Note that this is the first term that is not in A336547, as both sigma(3^2) and sigma(61^2) = 3*13*97 have 13 among their prime factors.
%e A401067 With n = 74505805908203125 = 305175781 * 5^12, sigma(5^12) = 305175781, thus dividing n, and therefore n is a term of this sequence, and incidentally, the only multiple of 5 among the initial 270 terms.
%e A401067 11825965688123277527713569 = A401388(23) * 262209281 =  3^4 * 7^2 * 11^2 * 19^2 * 127^4 * 262209281 is a term, one not in A319630.
%o A401067 (PARI) is_A401067(n) = if(!(n%2)||(omega(n)<2), 0, my(f=factor(n), y=0, s=1); for(i=1, #f~, if(1==(f[i, 2]%4), if((1==y)||(1!=(f[i, 1]%4)), return(0), y=1), if(f[i, 2]%2, return(0), s *= sigma(f[i,1]^f[i,2])))); (y && !(n%s)));
%o A401067 (PARI) list(lim) = {my(v = List(), f, rat, d, e, m, k); forstep(r = 3, sqrtint(lim), 2, f=factor(r); for(i = 1, #f~, f[i,2] *= 2); rat = r^2/sigma(f); d = denominator(rat); if(isprimepower(d, &p) && p % 4 == 1 && gcd(p, r) == 1, e = valuation(d, p); m = 1 + 4*((e-1)\4); k = r^2 * p^m; while(k <= lim, listput(v, k); k *= (p^4)))); Set(v);} \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 30 2026
%Y A401067 Intersection of A228058 and A401070.
%Y A401067 Cf. A005100, A319630, A336548, A350388, A351568, A401386, A401387, A401388.
%Y A401067 Contains more or less common terms with several similar sequences, for example, of the initial 270 terms, 263 are in A387406 and in A388276, 261 in A240991, 259 in A327165, 258 in A386429, 242 in A326064, nine in A336554, eight in A383482, seven in A001694 (and thus in A386428), one (117) in A336553, while none of them occur in A388269, in A325981, nor in A349753, and only two (117 and 92781) are found in A388277. Note that because of the dearth of terms with more than two prime factors among the initial terms (7 out of 270), this sampling is not very representative.
%K A401067 nonn,changed
%O A401067 1,1
%A A401067 _Antti Karttunen_, Oct 30 2026
%E A401067 More terms from _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 30 2026
